    {
      "scenario_id": "KA-07-false-alarm",
      "run_key": "master-tot-critic-off-run-2/4/KA-07-false-alarm",
      "expected_faults": [
        "false_alarm"
      ],
      "final_diagnosis": "CNC-01's vibration alarm (ticks 521-524, 7.01-7.37 mm/s) was a transient sensor/instrument disturbance, not a confirmed machine fault. Vibration spiked for exactly four ticks while spindle load (64.5-69.2%), temperature (122-126°F), power (~11.6-11.9 kW), and following error (3-5 µm) all stayed normal, then vibration returned to baseline ~2.2-2.4 mm/s at tick 525 and remained there through tick 530 with no recurrence. CNC-01 speed is unchanged at 1000 rpm. Alarms appear only on CNC-01; CNC-02 vibration was normal over the same window and the event log shows no other machine alarms. A real bearing fault would not self-recover in one tick with no load/temperature/power change, and benign job-8 variation does not explain a sustained 3x alarm-level spike isolated to one channel. Confidence ~0.80, below the 0.90 threshold for an intervene; moreover none of the available human-approved actions (replace_bearing, reset_setting, replace_servo_drive, repair_hydraulics) addresses a vibration-sensor transient, so no change is indicated. Monitor CNC-01 and inspect the vibration sensor if similar spikes recur; an inspection report confirming sensor health would raise confidence further.",
